meta {
  name: Place the whole book on the curriculum
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/placements
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "curriculum_id": "{{curriculumId}}",
    "content_item_id": "{{libraryItemId}}",
    "grain": "curriculum",
    "origin": "inferred",
    "confidence": 0.72
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.grain: eq curriculum
  res.body.origin: eq inferred
  res.body.status: eq proposed
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("wholeBookPlacementId", res.body.id);
}

docs {
  The coarse mapping: this book belongs to this syllabus, without saying where
  in it. It is what an extraction pipeline can assert from a title page alone,
  which is why this one is origin inferred and carries a confidence.

  Grain is the difference between the two. curriculum grain must not carry a
  node -- the database enforces that with a check constraint as well as the
  API, since a row that says "whole syllabus" while naming one topic is two
  claims that cannot both be true.

  Coarse placements are how a query about a topic nobody mapped still finds
  something: resolve with include_ancestors=true and this row answers for every
  node in the syllabus, marked exact false so the caller knows it was a
  fallback. Request 9 shows that.

  confidence is only meaningful next to origin inferred. Nothing rejects a
  confidence on a manual placement, but a person who typed a mapping and gave
  it 0.7 has said nothing a reader can act on.
}
